# [Determine if Two Strings Are Close](https://leetcode.com/problems/determine-if-two-strings-are-close/)

## Approach
Two strings are considered close if we can transform one string into the other using the following operations:

1. Swap any two existing characters.
2. Transform every occurrence of one character into another character and vice versa.

To determine whether two strings are close, we check three conditions:

- **Equal Length:** Both strings must have the same length.
- **Same Characters:** Both strings must contain exactly the same set of distinct characters.
- **Same Frequency Distribution:** The frequencies of characters in both strings must match after sorting the frequency arrays.

The first two conditions ensure that we cannot introduce a new character or remove an existing character. Sorting the frequency arrays verifies that the character frequencies can be rearranged to match using the allowed operations.

## Algorithm
1. If the lengths of the strings differ, return `false`.
2. Create two frequency arrays of size 26 to count lowercase English letters.
3. Traverse both strings and calculate the frequency of each character.
4. Check that both strings contain the same characters. If a character appears in only one string, return `false`.
5. Sort both frequency arrays.
6. Return whether the sorted frequency arrays are equal.

## C++ Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool closeStrings(string word1, string word2) {
        if (word1.length() != word2.length()) return false;

        vector<int> alpha1(26, 0), alpha2(26, 0);

        for (char ch : word1) alpha1[ch - 'a']++;
        for (char ch : word2) alpha2[ch - 'a']++;

        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if ((alpha1[i] == 0 && alpha2[i] > 0) ||
                (alpha2[i] == 0 && alpha1[i] > 0)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        sort(alpha1.begin(), alpha1.end());
        sort(alpha2.begin(), alpha2.end());

        return alpha1 == alpha2;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n)\), where \(n\) is the length of the strings. Counting frequencies takes \(O(n)\), while sorting two fixed-size arrays of 26 elements takes constant time.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(1)\), since both frequency arrays have a fixed size of 26.

## Key Takeaway
Two strings are close if they have the **same set of characters and the same multiset of character frequencies**. The frequencies can belong to different characters because the allowed operations let us swap character identities.
